# Comptime is fun time

For a recent project we had to write a columnar CSV parser with a
fixed schema and decided to use Zig for this.

The CSV files we were supposed to read were all with a set of
compile-time known schemas, about 13 different ones. After parsing we
needed to efficiently access the records in a columnar way. We could
not use a columnar format like Arrow, we had to read CSV files.

## Schema

We decided to use `MultiArrayList` to represent slices of records in a
columnar way. In our case some of the data we dealt with encoded
linear constraints we passed to solvers like HiGHs or Gurobi.

```zig

// Each row is in COO (coordinate list).
const SparseMatrixRow = struct {
    row: u64,
    col: u64,
    value: f64,
};

const SparseMatrix = MultiArrayList(SparseMatrixRow);
```

The record type is our schema. We want to compile a specialized reader
for this particular schema, parsing CSV records and returning or
filling a `MultiArrayList`.

We want to support a subset of primitive types in Zig, notably also
optionals.

## Tokenizer

We implement a simple tokenizer (heavily inspired by the Zig languages
compiler tokenizer).

```zig

const Tokenizer = struct {
    buffer: [:0]const u8,
    index: usize,

    // Each token has a tag and a location in the source.
    const Token = struct {
        tag: Tag,
        loc: Loc,

        // Index points into the source where the value of this token
        // starts.
        const Loc = struct {
            start: usize,
            end: usize,
        };

        // Kind of token.
        const Tag = enum {
            field,
            delimiter,
            clrf,
            invalid,
            eof,
        };
    };
```

The tokznier contains a zero-terminated slice, the CSV file we are
reading, and it produces tokens. Each token points to some data in the
buffer and has a tag. When looking at
[rfc4180](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4180.html) we know that a
valid CSV file does not have so many different tokens.

The tokenizer reads each byte from the source and we have a simple
state machine to decide what kind of token we are dealing with:

```zig
    // State of the tokenizer.
    const State = enum {
        start,
        eof,
        quote,
        field,
        delimiter,
        clrf,
        invalid,
    };

    // Produce the next token.
    pub fn next(self: *Tokenizer) Token {
        var result: Token = .{
            .tag = .field,
            .loc = .{ .start = self.index, .end = undefined },
        };

        state: switch (State.start) {
            .start => {
                switch (self.buffer[self.index]) {
                    '"' => {
                        // Quoted fields should not contain the quote
                        // itself.
                        result.loc.start = self.index + 1;
                        continue :state .quote;
                    },
                    // ... other cases ...
            },
            // ... other states ...
        }
        return result;
};
```
We read on byte after the other and use Zigs new `continue :state` to
do a state transition. The statemachine encodes the CSV syntax. The
only way for us to go into the `.invalid` state is when a plain `.field`
contains a quote. That is not allowed. 

## Reader

Now the reader is a generic type specialized for the record type. 

```zig

///  A ReaderType parses CSVs and returns a MultiArrayList of records.
pub fn ReaderType(comptime Record: type) type {
    return struct {
        inner: *Io.Reader,

        const Reader = @This();
        const RecordList = std.MultiArrayList(Record);
        // ... implementation ...
        
        /// Parses a row into the Record struct.
        fn parseRecord(tokenizer: *Tokenizer, source: []const u8) !?Record {
        }
```

`parseRecord` is where the comptime starts. From the provided record
type we statically know how many fields we expect and what types. We
can use `comptime` to unroll the parsing loop, creating an optimal but
also maintainable functionality.

```zig
/// Parses a row into the Record struct.
fn parseRecord(tokenizer: *Tokenizer, source: []const u8) !?Record {
    var record: Record = undefined;
    const columns = @typeInfo(Record).@"struct".fields;
    inline for (columns, 0..) |column, idx| {
        const token = tokenizer.next();
        switch (token.tag) {
            .eof => return null,
            .field => {
                
            },
        }
    }
```

